  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This perspective article has an early but visible scholarly footprint: it has one OpenAlex citation and one documented downstream reuse. The record suggests that its mechanistic framing of amphibian climate vulnerability has already been taken up by another research article, while broader attention remains limited so far.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in <i>Functional Ecology</i> in 2026, this open-access perspective by Eric A. Riddell, D. R. Adams, and Molly C. Womack examines why amphibians are often regarded as highly vulnerable to climate change and argues that the key issue is mechanistic understanding. Rather than treating vulnerability as a general trait, the article evaluates the physiological pathways most often used to explain sensitivity and emphasizes that forecasts are only as reliable as the assumptions about how amphibians actually experience environmental variation in nature.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The impact record is still small, but it is already concrete. OpenAlex shows one citation, and the reuse graph reports one downstream reuse in a research article. That citing paper, published in <i>Integrative and Comparative Biology</i>, indicates uptake within comparative and amphibian biology, consistent with the article\u2019s focus on behavioral and physiological responses in climate-related contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Because the citation base is still very limited, the strongest reading is one of early scholarly engagement rather than established field-wide influence. The available evidence points to a conceptually useful paper that is being noticed by researchers working on amphibian biology and related ecological questions, but the record does not yet support broader claims about sustained reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals remain modest. The article is gold open access and is listed in repository holdings, which should support access and reuse, and there is one recorded blog mention in a general ecology forum. These traces suggest some outward visibility, but the main documented impact at this stage is the emergence of a small citation trail around the paper\u2019s mechanistic approach to climate vulnerability in amphibians.</p>",
